---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Research Interests" sideBarActiveItemID="research-interests">
  <div class="mb-5 text-center">
    <div class="text-5xl w-full font-bold">Research Interests</div>
  </div>

  <div class="mx-auto mt-4 mb-4 h-[2px] w-7/8 bg-white opacity-80"></div>

  <div class="space-y-20">
    <!-- Intro -->
    <section>
      <div class="text-center px-4 md:px-10 xl:px-16">
        <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-4xl mx-auto">
          Broadly, my research centers on how cognitive priorities change across the lifespan and how different sources of social learning shape children's learning, effort, and production
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Cognitive Priorities section -->
    <section>
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6 mt-4 text-center px-4 md:px-10 xl:px-16">
        Cognitive Priorities Across the Lifespan
      </h2>

      <div class="mx-auto mt-4 mb-4 h-[2px] w-7/8 bg-white opacity-80"></div>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-[30%_70%] gap-8 items-center">
        <!-- Left: Image -->
        <div class="pl-4 md:pl-10 xl:pl-16">
          <img
            src="/great_ideas.webp"
            alt="Cognitive Priorities Across the Lifespan"
            class="w-full object-contain"
          />
        </div>

        <!-- Right: Text -->
        <div class="text-left leading-relaxed px-4 md:px-10 xl:px-16">
          <p>
            This line of work examines the cognitive priorities that guide learning, exploration, and action across development. I am especially interested in when human cognition privileges efficiency, when it favors exploration, and how these priorities shift across the lifespan.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-2 mt-5">Current Questions</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>Do children exploit when they care about the goal?</li>
            <li>Is speed–accuracy integration a core feature of human cognition or a WEIRD phenomenon?</li>
            <li>How is generativity conventionalized across the lifespan?</li>
            <li>Is “resetting” an intuitive heuristic that allows children to be more exploratory in their production?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mx-auto mt-8 mb-4 h-[2px] w-7/8 bg-white opacity-80"></div>
    </section>

    <!-- Evolving Sources of Social Learning section -->
    <section>
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6 text-center px-4 md:px-10 xl:px-16">
        Evolving Sources of Social Learning
      </h2>

      <div class="mx-auto mt-4 mb-4 h-[2px] w-7/8 bg-white opacity-80"></div>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-[30%_70%] gap-8 items-center">
        <!-- Left: Image -->
        <div class="pl-4 md:pl-10 xl:pl-16">
          <img
            src="/acute_changes.webp"
            alt="Evolving Sources of Social Learning"
            class="w-full object-contain"
          />
        </div>

        <!-- Right: Text -->
        <div class="text-left leading-relaxed px-4 md:px-10 xl:px-16">
          <p>
            This line of work focuses on how learning changes as a function of who provides information and how that information is presented. I am particularly interested in how children learn from adults versus peers, and in how instructional contexts shape persistence, exploration, and discovery.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-2 mt-5">Current Questions</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>How do children respond when instructions go away, and how is that influenced by how an adult initially presents those instructions?</li>
            <li>How do children learn differently from peer versus adult instruction?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="flex justify-center w-full relative mt-0">
        <img
          src="/moon_short.webp"
          alt="Thanks for coming!"
          class="h-90 md:h-95 object-contain w-[95%] md:w-[100%] max-w-[1000px] z-0"
        />
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>